Analyze the following C# code:

public void StartProcessing() {
    var task = new Task(async () => {
        await Task.Delay(1000);
        SaveToDatabase();
    });
    task.Start();
    task.Wait();
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain why SaveToDatabase() executes after task.Wait() finishes and what problem arises when using async inside the Task constructor.
2. Refactor the method to use async/await properly without 'new Task', 'Start', or 'Wait'.

Analyze this C# Singleton code processing concurrent tasks:

public class WorkerService {
    private readonly SemaphoreSlim _semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim(5);
    private readonly List<string> _results = new List<string>();
    public async Task ProcessItemsAsync(List<string> items) {
        var tasks = items.Select(async item => {
            _semaphore.Wait();
            var processed = await TransformAsync(item);
            _results.Add(processed);
            _semaphore.Release();
        });
        Task.WaitAll(tasks.ToArray());
    }
    private async Task<string> TransformAsync(string input) => await Task.FromResult(input.ToUpper());
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
Explicitly mention the 4 errors and their solutions in the text:
1. Replace 'Task.WaitAll' with 'Task.WhenAll'.
2. Replace 'Wait()' with 'WaitAsync()' on the semaphore.
3. Protect the non-thread-safe 'List<T>' (using lock or ConcurrentBag).
4. Wrap 'Release()' inside a 'try/finally' block.

